@seabass-labrax Thanks for the feedback. The intention behind this license is to provide a tool for projects to clarify who is allowed to use its name under which conditions. I have seen so many sustainability initiatives or software projects that "lost" their name simply because they did not care about it. When some other initiative or a company found it nice or just by accident used the same name that conflict situation caused to cease the whole initiative.

To protect a trademark there are quite clear international rules and registries for most regions in the world. Agree that it may be difficult to decide if a word or a combined term is eligible to be registered  and in which class and  there are various best practices to share a brand used in different regions or application areas.

First of all, the Label Commons Public License is a tool for making clear, that the trademark issue has been taken attention and that some means have been or may be undertaken, to protect it in some regions in some classes. There will be never an unconditional sharing offer, it remains a joint effort of all users of the brand  to protect it  and to support a watchdog organisation to take appropriate measures, which can vary from just communicating that it is being in use (which makes it harder for anybody else to claim it) up to worldwide trademark registration.

In comparison to the SPDX Trademark License 1.1 the LCPL is sub-licensable, which may give another organisation in another region the option to take measures to protect the brand with a local registry. We probably should make that option more clear.  In fact the LCPL tries to offer a template for what SPDX tried to clarify with its trademark license.

@richardfontana  LCPL has been presented at a large international event for media and art in 2019 and honestly we do not know how many initiatives use it. The promoting non-profit organisation fairkom has recently launched a  Mastodon instance fairmove.net to vitalize the usage of the fairmove brand, which has been showcased at several occasions as a generic label for sustainability and activism.
